    "question": {
      ":question/id": "aa814591-b1ca-5960-8205-3b5374dd826d",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{x+7}+\\frac{1}{x-6})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{x+7}+\\frac{1}{x-6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+7}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x+7|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x-6|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|(x+7)(x-6)|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|(x+7)(x-6)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm.",
